              title: "The number at the bottom",
              source: "From a briefing by a London workers' advice charity (adapted)",
              text: "Most people in Britain can name their monthly salary. Far fewer can name their [[take-home pay|líquido]] without turning the [[payslip|nómina]] over. That gap is not a private failing. It is the result of a document that was designed by payroll software, not by a reader, and of a culture that still treats pay as a secret even when the law has started to treat it as information.<br/><br/>This briefing does not argue that everyone should discuss their salary at lunch. It sets out what the numbers on a UK payslip actually mean, and what has changed, quietly, in the last few years.<br/><br/>The top figure is [[gross pay|bruto]]. It is the contractual amount before anything is taken off. Below it sit the deductions. Income tax is the one people expect. [[National Insurance|la Seguridad Social británica]] is the one they ring the agency about, convinced there has been a [[delay in payment|retraso en el pago]]. There has not. NI is a deduction with another name, and it is not optional. Pension contributions, where they exist, sit on the same list. What remains is net pay, also called take-home pay: the figure that hits the bank.<br/><br/>A 2024 survey of workers in hospitality and social care in Greater London found that two in five could not identify the NI line on their own payslip, and that one in three had, at least once, compared the gross figure with a colleague's net figure and concluded they were being underpaid. They were often wrong. They were also, the briefing notes, not foolish. The document invites the mistake.<br/><br/>[[Pay transparency|transparencia salarial]] has a narrower meaning in law than it has in columns. From 2026, larger employers in Britain are being pushed to publish more about the range of pay for a role, and some job adverts now carry a salary band as a matter of course. The briefing records this as a fact. It does not say the bands are generous, and it does not say they are honest. It says they exist, and that a candidate who cannot read a band is at a disadvantage that has nothing to do with talent.<br/><br/>What would actually [[ease the workload|aliviar la carga]] of the first month in a new post is smaller and less glamorous. A one-page key to the payslip, written in the language of the people who receive it. A line in the [[job description|descripción del puesto]] that states the hours, the overtime rate, and whether December runs longer. A named person to ask, in writing, when a deduction appears that was not there the month before. None of this requires a campaign. All of it requires a sentence that does one job.<br/><br/>The last number is the one the briefing will not dress up. In inner London, the [[going rate|lo que se está pagando]] for part-time advice-desk work sits well below what a full-time bus driver earns with overtime, and well above what a café pays for the same number of hours. That is not a moral ranking. It is a map. People who cannot read the map spend their first three months angry at the wrong figure.<br/><br/>A further practical point, because the first month is where most of the anger sits. New starters often have a [[probation period|periodo de prueba]] of three or six months, during which hours can still move and overtime is not always in the contract. The briefing's advice is unglamorous and the same in every sector it has looked at: photograph the job description on day one, keep every payslip, and put questions to payroll in writing. A sentence that does one job is worth more, in that first month, than a conversation on a bus about who earns more. The bus will still be there on Friday. The wrong figure, once it has been believed, is harder to unsay.",
